        "# Lecture 22.2: Weak Convergence over $R^d$\n",
        "\n",
        "# Overview & Recapitulation\n",
        "\n",
        "Recall from the previous lecture the **Portmanteau Theorem** for a metric space $(S,d)$:\n",
        "For $\\mu_n, \\mu \\in \\mathcal{P}(S, \\mathcal{B}(S))$, the following are equivalent:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Definition**: $\\int_S f \\, d\\mu_n \\to \\int_S f \\, d\\mu \\quad \\forall f \\in C_b(S)$ ($\\mu_n \\implies \\mu$).\n",
        "2. **Lipschitz functions**: $\\int_S f \\, d\\mu_n \\to \\int_S f \\, d\\mu \\quad \\forall f \\in \\text{Lip}_b(S)$.\n",
        "3. **Upper bound on closed sets**: $\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty} \\mu_n(F) \\le \\mu(F) \\quad \\forall \\text{ closed } F \\subseteq S$.\n",
        "4. **Lower bound on open sets**: $\\liminf_{n\\to\\infty} \\mu_n(G) \\ge \\mu(G) \\quad \\forall \\text{ open } G \\subseteq S$.\n",
        "5. **Continuity sets**: $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\mu_n(A) = \\mu(A) \\quad \\forall \\mu\\text{-continuity sets } A \\in \\mathcal{B}(S)$.\n",
        "\n",
        "In this lecture, we restrict $S = \\mathbb{R}^d$ and establish **three additional equivalent** characterizations of weak convergence using smaller classes of test functions ($C_c$, $C^\\infty_c$) and distribution functions (CDF convergence).\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Weak Convergence via Continuous Functions with Compact Support\n",
        "\n",
        "Testing against all bounded continuous functions $C_b(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ can be demanding. We show that it suffices to check test functions in $C_c(\\mathbb{R}^d)$, the space of continuous functions with compact support:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "C_c(\\mathbb{R}^d) = \\{ f \\in C(\\mathbb{R}^d) : \\text{supp}(f) \\subseteq K \\text{ for some compact } K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^d \\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{#lem-tightness-balls}\n",
        "### Lemma: Asymptotic Concentration on Bounded Balls\n",
        "If $\\mu_n \\implies \\mu$ on $\\mathbb{R}^d$ (or if $\\int f \\, d\\mu_n \\to \\int f \\, d\\mu$ for all $f \\in C_c(\\mathbb{R}^d)$), then:\n",
        "$$\\lim_{R \\to \\infty} \\inf_{n} \\mu_n(\\bar{B}_R) = 1$$\n",
        "where $\\bar{B}_R = \\{ x \\in \\mathbb{R}^d : |x| \\le R \\}$ is the closed ball of radius $R$.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{.proof}\n",
        "## Proof of @lem-tightness-balls\n",
        "\n",
        "Only the weaker hypothesis is used: we assume $\\int f \\, d\\mu_n \\to \\int f \\, d\\mu$ for every $f \\in C_c(\\mathbb{R}^d)$.\n",
        "\n",
        "For $R > 0$, construct a continuous cutoff function $\\psi_R \\in C_c(\\mathbb{R}^d)$:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\psi_R(x) = g_R(|x|), \\quad \\text{where } g_R(t) = \n",
        "\\begin{cases} \n",
        "1, & t \\le R/2, \\\\ \n",
        "1 - \\frac{t - R/2}{R/2}, & R/2 \\le t \\le R, \\\\ \n",
        "0, & t > R. \n",
        "\\end{cases}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "It is continuous because $g_R$ is piecewise linear with matching values at $t = R/2$ and $t = R$, and $x \\mapsto |x|$ is continuous; its support lies in the compact set $\\bar{B}_R$.\n",
        "\n",
        "Using Python, we can generate the plot of $g_R(t)$ for $R = 10$:"
      ],
