      "source": [
        "This satisfies $\\mathbf{1}_{\\bar{B}_{R/2}} \\le \\psi_R \\le \\mathbf{1}_{\\bar{B}_R}$: indeed $\\psi_R = 1$ on $\\bar B_{R/2}$, $0 \\le \\psi_R \\le 1$ everywhere, and $\\psi_R = 0$ off $\\bar B_R$.\n",
        "Integrating gives:\n",
        "$$\\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} \\psi_R \\, d\\mu_n \\le \\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} \\mathbf{1}_{\\bar{B}_R} \\, d\\mu_n = \\mu_n(\\bar{B}_R)$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Since $\\psi_R \\in C_c(\\mathbb{R}^d)$, the hypothesis gives $\\int \\psi_R \\, d\\mu_n \\to \\int \\psi_R \\, d\\mu$. Taking $\\liminf_{n \\to \\infty}$ on both sides:\n",
        "$$\\liminf_{n \\to \\infty} \\mu_n(\\bar{B}_R) \\ge \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} \\psi_R \\, d\\mu_n = \\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} \\psi_R \\, d\\mu \\ge \\mu(\\bar{B}_{R/2})$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Since $\\bar{B}_{R/2} \\uparrow \\mathbb{R}^d$ as $R \\to \\infty$, continuity from below gives $\\mu(\\bar{B}_{R/2}) \\uparrow 1$.\n",
        "\n",
        "**From $\\liminf_n$ to $\\inf_n$.** Fix $\\epsilon > 0$. Choose $R_0$ with $\\mu(\\bar{B}_{R_0/2}) > 1 - \\epsilon/2$, so that $\\liminf_{n\\to\\infty}\\mu_n(\\bar{B}_{R_0}) \\ge 1 - \\epsilon/2 > 1 - \\epsilon$. Hence there is $N$ with\n",
        "$$\\mu_n(\\bar{B}_{R_0}) > 1 - \\epsilon \\qquad \\text{for all } n \\ge N.$$\n",
        "The remaining indices $n = 1, \\dots, N-1$ are finitely many, and for each of them $\\mu_n(\\bar{B}_r) \\uparrow 1$ as $r \\to \\infty$, so we may pick $R_n$ with $\\mu_n(\\bar{B}_{R_n}) > 1 - \\epsilon$. Setting $R^* := \\max\\{R_0, R_1, \\dots, R_{N-1}\\}$ and using monotonicity of $r \\mapsto \\mu_n(\\bar B_r)$:\n",
        "$$\\inf_{n} \\mu_n(\\bar{B}_{R^*}) \\ge 1 - \\epsilon.$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The map $R \\mapsto \\inf_n \\mu_n(\\bar{B}_R)$ is nondecreasing and bounded by $1$, so its limit as $R \\to \\infty$ exists and is at least $1 - \\epsilon$. As $\\epsilon > 0$ was arbitrary,\n",
        "$$\\lim_{R \\to \\infty} \\inf_n \\mu_n(\\bar{B}_R) = 1. \\qquad \\blacksquare$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{#thm-cc-convergence}\n",
        "## $C_c(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ Characterization\n",
        "Let $\\mu_n, \\mu \\in \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{R}^d, \\mathcal{B}(\\mathbb{R}^d))$. Then $\\mu_n \\implies \\mu$ if and only if:\n",
        "$$\\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} f \\, d\\mu_n \\longrightarrow \\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} f \\, d\\mu \\quad \\forall f \\in C_c(\\mathbb{R}^d)$$\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{.proof}\n",
        "## Proof of @thm-cc-convergence\n",
        "\n",
        "- $(\\implies)$: Immediate since $C_c(\\mathbb{R}^d) \\subseteq C_b(\\mathbb{R}^d)$.\n",
        "- $(\\impliedby)$: Let $f \\in C_b(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ with $\\|f\\|_\\infty < \\infty$. Truncate $f$ using the cutoff function $\\psi_R \\in C_c(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ defined in @lem-tightness-balls:\n",
        "  $$f_R(x) = f(x) \\cdot \\psi_R(x) \\in C_c(\\mathbb{R}^d)$$\n",
        "\n",
        "  We decompose the integration error using a three-term triangle inequality:\n",
        "  $$\\begin{aligned}\n",
        "  \\left| \\int f d\\mu - \\int f d\\mu_n \\right| &\\le \\left| \\int f d\\mu - \\int f_R d\\mu \\right| + \\left| \\int f_R d\\mu - \\int f_R d\\mu_n \\right| \\\\\n",
        "  &\\quad + \\left| \\int f_R d\\mu_n - \\int f d\\mu_n \\right|\n",
        "  \\end{aligned}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "  Taking $\\limsup_{n \\to \\infty}$ on both sides:\n",
        "  $$\\begin{aligned}\n",
        "  \\limsup_{n\\to\\infty} \\left| \\int f d\\mu - \\int f d\\mu_n \\right| &\\le \\int |f - f_R| \\, d\\mu + 0 + \\limsup_{n\\to\\infty} \\int |f - f_R| \\, d\\mu_n\n",
        "  \\end{aligned}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "  For the tail integrals, note that $|f - f_R| = |f|\\,(1 - \\psi_R)$ and that $1 - \\psi_R$ vanishes on $\\bar{B}_{R/2}$, so $1 - \\psi_R \\le \\mathbf{1}_{\\bar{B}_{R/2}^c}$. Hence, for $\\nu \\in \\{\\mu, \\mu_1, \\mu_2, \\dots\\}$:\n",
        "  $$\\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} |f - f_R| \\, d\\nu \\le \\|f\\|_\\infty \\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} (1 - \\psi_R) \\, d\\nu \\le \\|f\\|_\\infty \\left( 1 - \\nu(\\bar{B}_{R/2}) \\right)$$\n",
        "\n",
        "  Applying this to $\\mu$ and to each $\\mu_n$, the previous display becomes, for every $R > 0$:\n",
        "  $$\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty} \\left| \\int f d\\mu - \\int f d\\mu_n \\right| \\le \\|f\\|_\\infty \\bigl( 1 - \\mu(\\bar{B}_{R/2}) \\bigr) + \\|f\\|_\\infty \\Bigl( 1 - \\inf_n \\mu_n(\\bar{B}_{R/2}) \\Bigr)$$\n",
        "\n",
        "  The left-hand side is a single number that does not depend on $R$. Letting $R \\to \\infty$, the first term tends to $0$ by continuity from below, and the second tends to $0$ by @lem-tightness-balls (whose hypothesis, convergence on $C_c(\\mathbb{R}^d)$, is exactly what we are assuming). Therefore the left-hand side equals $0$, i.e.\n",
        "  $$\\int f \\, d\\mu_n \\longrightarrow \\int f \\, d\\mu \\quad \\text{for all } f \\in C_b(\\mathbb{R}^d). \\qquad \\blacksquare$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Weak Convergence via Smooth Test Functions\n",
        "\n",
        "We can further reduce the test function class to smooth functions with compact support, $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d)$:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d) = \\{ f \\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d) : \\text{supp}(f) \\subseteq K \\text{ for some compact } K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^d \\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{#cor-smooth-convergence}\n",
        "### Corollary: $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ Characterization\n",
        "Let $\\mu_n, \\mu \\in \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{R}^d, \\mathcal{B}(\\mathbb{R}^d))$. Then $\\mu_n \\implies \\mu$ if and only if:\n",
        "$$\\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} f \\, d\\mu_n \\longrightarrow \\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} f \\, d\\mu \\quad \\forall f \\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d)$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{.proof}\n",
        "## Proof of @cor-smooth-convergence\n",
        "\n",
        "- $(\\implies)$: Since $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d) \\subseteq C_b(\\mathbb{R}^d)$, this direction is immediate from the definition of weak convergence.\n",
        "- $(\\impliedby)$: By @thm-cc-convergence, it suffices to show that convergence over $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ implies convergence over $C_c(\\mathbb{R}^d)$.\n",
        "\n",
        "Fix $f \\in C_c(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ supported on a compact set $K \\subset \\mathbb{R}^d$. Fix a **standard mollifier**: a function $\\rho \\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ with\n",
        "$$\\rho \\ge 0, \\qquad \\text{supp}(\\rho) \\subseteq \\bar{B}_1(0), \\qquad \\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} \\rho(u)\\,du = 1,$$\n",
        "and set $\\rho_\\epsilon(x) := \\epsilon^{-d} \\rho(x/\\epsilon)$, so that $\\rho_\\epsilon \\ge 0$, $\\text{supp}(\\rho_\\epsilon) \\subseteq \\bar{B}_\\epsilon(0)$ and $\\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} \\rho_\\epsilon(u) \\, du = 1$ by the change of variables $u = \\epsilon v$.\n",
        "Define the mollified function:\n",
        "$$f_\\epsilon(x) = (f * \\rho_\\epsilon)(x) = \\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} f(x - u) \\rho_\\epsilon(u) \\, du = \\int_{\\bar B_\\epsilon(0)} f(x - u) \\rho_\\epsilon(u) \\, du$$\n",
        "\n",
        "- $f_\\epsilon \\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d)$: writing $f_\\epsilon(x) = \\int f(y)\\rho_\\epsilon(x - y)\\,dy$, the integrand is smooth in $x$ with all derivatives dominated by $\\|f\\|_\\infty \\sup|\\partial^\\alpha \\rho_\\epsilon|\\,\\mathbf{1}_K(y)$, so differentiation under the integral sign is justified and $\\partial^\\alpha f_\\epsilon = f * \\partial^\\alpha \\rho_\\epsilon$. Its support is contained in the compact set $K + \\bar{B}_\\epsilon$, since $f(x-u) = 0$ unless $x \\in K + u$.\n",
        "- Since $f \\in C_c(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ is continuous with compact support, it is uniformly continuous on $\\mathbb{R}^d$. Using $f(x) = \\int_{\\bar B_\\epsilon(0)} f(x)\\rho_\\epsilon(u)\\,du$ (valid because $\\rho_\\epsilon$ integrates to $1$) and $\\rho_\\epsilon \\ge 0$:\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "  \\begin{align*}\n",
        "  \\|f - f_\\epsilon\\|_\\infty &= \\sup_{x \\in \\mathbb{R}^d} \\left| \\int_{\\bar B_\\epsilon(0)} (f(x) - f(x-u)) \\rho_\\epsilon(u) \\, du \\right|\\\\\n",
        "  &\\le \\sup_{x \\in \\mathbb{R}^d} \\int_{\\bar B_\\epsilon(0)} |f(x) - f(x-u)| \\, \\rho_\\epsilon(u) \\, du \\\\\n",
        "  &\\le \\left(\\sup_{x \\in \\mathbb{R}^d} \\sup_{|u| \\le \\epsilon} |f(x) - f(x-u)|\\right) \\underbrace{\\int_{\\bar B_\\epsilon(0)} \\rho_\\epsilon(u) \\, du}_{=1} \\xrightarrow{\\epsilon \\to 0} 0\n",
        "  \\end{align*}\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "  by uniform continuity of $f$.\n",
        "\n",
        "Using a three-term inequality, and noting that $\\mu_n, \\mu$ are probability measures so $\\int |g| \\, d\\nu \\le \\|g\\|_\\infty$ for any probability measure $\\nu$:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\begin{aligned}\n",
        "\\left| \\int f \\, d\\mu_n - \\int f \\, d\\mu \\right| &\\le \\left| \\int f \\, d\\mu_n - \\int f_\\epsilon \\, d\\mu_n \\right| + \\left| \\int f_\\epsilon \\, d\\mu_n - \\int f_\\epsilon \\, d\\mu \\right| \\\\\n",
        "&\\quad + \\left| \\int f_\\epsilon \\, d\\mu - \\int f \\, d\\mu \\right| \\\\\n",
        "&\\le \\|f - f_\\epsilon\\|_\\infty + \\left| \\int f_\\epsilon \\, d\\mu_n - \\int f_\\epsilon \\, d\\mu \\right| + \\|f - f_\\epsilon\\|_\\infty.\n",
        "\\end{aligned}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Taking $\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}$: since $f_\\epsilon \\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ and convergence over $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ is assumed, the middle term vanishes. Thus:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty} \\left| \\int f \\, d\\mu_n - \\int f \\, d\\mu \\right| \\le 2 \\|f - f_\\epsilon\\|_\\infty \\qquad \\text{for every } \\epsilon > 0.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The left-hand side does not depend on $\\epsilon$, and the right-hand side tends to $0$ as $\\epsilon \\to 0$; hence the left-hand side is $0$. This proves $\\int f \\, d\\mu_n \\to \\int f \\, d\\mu$ for all $f \\in C_c(\\mathbb{R}^d)$, and therefore $\\mu_n \\implies \\mu$ by @thm-cc-convergence. $\\blacksquare$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Convergence of Cumulative Distribution Functions (CDFs) on $\\mathbb{R}$\n",
        "\n",
        "Every probability measure $\\mu$ on $(\\mathbb{R}, \\mathcal{B}(\\mathbb{R}))$ uniquely corresponds to a right-continuous cumulative distribution function (CDF):\n",
        "$$F(t) = \\mu((-\\infty, t])$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For a function $F: S \\to T$, let $\\text{Cont}(F)$ denote the set of continuity points of $F$, and $\\text{Disc}(F) = S \\setminus \\text{Cont}(F)$ denote its discontinuity points.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{#exm-dirac-cdf}\n",
        "## CDF Convergence Failure at Discontinuity Points\n",
        "Let $a_n = a + \\frac{1}{n} \\to a$ in $\\mathbb{R}$. The corresponding measures are $\\mu_n = \\delta_{a_n}$ and $\\mu = \\delta_a$.\n",
        "Their CDFs are:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "F_n(t) = \\delta_{a_n}((-\\infty, t]) = \n",
        "\\begin{cases} \n",
        "1, & t \\ge a_n \\\\ \n",
        "0, & t < a_n \n",
        "\\end{cases} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad F(t) = \n",
        "\\begin{cases}\n",
        " 1, & t \\ge a \\\\\n",
        " 0, & t < a \n",
        "\\end{cases}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Evaluating at $t = a$:\n",
        "\n",
        "- $F_n(a) = \\mathbf{1}_{\\{a_n \\le a\\}} = 0$ for all $n \\ge 1$.\n",
        "- $F(a) = \\mathbf{1}_{\\{a \\le a\\}} = 1$.\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus $F_n(a) \\to 0 \\neq F(a) = 1$.\n",
        "However, $t = a$ is a discontinuity point of $F$ ($a \\notin \\text{Cont}(F)$).\n",
        "For all $t \\neq a$, $F_n(t) \\to F(t)$ holds.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{#thm-cdf-convergence}\n",
        "## CDF Characterization of Weak Convergence on $\\mathbb{R}$\n",
        "Let $\\mu_n, \\mu \\in \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{R})$ with CDFs $F_n(t) = \\mu_n((-\\infty, t])$ and $F(t) = \\mu((-\\infty, t])$.\n",
        "Then $\\mu_n \\implies \\mu$ if and only if:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "F_n(t) \\longrightarrow F(t) \\quad \\forall t \\in \\text{Cont}(F).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{.proof}\n",
        "## Proof of @thm-cdf-convergence\n",
        "\n",
        "- $(\\implies)$:\n",
        "  Let $t \\in \\text{Cont}(F)$. Then $\\mu(\\{t\\}) = F(t) - F(t^-) = 0$.\n",
        "  The boundary of $A = (-\\infty, t]$ is $\\partial A = \\{t\\}$.\n",
        "  Since $\\mu(\\partial A) = \\mu(\\{t\\}) = 0$, $A$ is a $\\mu$-continuity set.\n",
        "  By Portmanteau Theorem (Condition 5):\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "  F_n(t) = \\mu_n((-\\infty, t]) \\longrightarrow \\mu((-\\infty, t]) = F(t).\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "\n",
        "- $(\\impliedby)$:\n",
        "  Since $F$ is a non-decreasing function on $\\mathbb{R}$, its set of discontinuities $\\text{Disc}(F)$ is at most countable. Thus $\\text{Cont}(F)$ is dense in $\\mathbb{R}$.\n",
        "\n",
        "  Let $f \\in C_c(\\mathbb{R})$ with support $\\text{supp}(f) \\subseteq [a, b]$.\n",
        "  Since $f$ is continuous with compact support, it is **uniformly continuous** on $\\mathbb{R}$.\n",
        "  Fix $\\epsilon > 0$. Choose $\\delta > 0$ such that $|x - y| < \\delta \\implies |f(x) - f(y)| < \\epsilon/2$.\n",
        "\n",
        "  Using density of $\\text{Cont}(F)$, construct a partition $\\pi = \\{x_0 < x_1 < \\dots < x_k\\}$ such that:\n",
        "\n",
        "  1. $x_j \\in \\text{Cont}(F)$ for all $j = 0, 1, \\dots, k$.\n",
        "  2. $|x_j - x_{j-1}| < \\delta$ for all $j = 1, \\dots, k$.\n",
        "  3. $x_0 < a$ and $x_k > b$, so that $[a,b] \\subseteq (x_0, x_k]$.\n",
        "\n",
        "  Condition 3 matters because $a$ and $b$ themselves may lie in $\\text{Disc}(F)$ and so may be unusable as partition points; density of $\\text{Cont}(F)$ lets us instead pick $x_0$ slightly below $a$ and $x_k$ slightly above $b$.\n",
        "\n",
        "  Define a step function approximation $f_\\pi$:\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "  f_\\pi(x) = \\sum_{j=1}^k f(x_j) \\mathbf{1}_{(x_{j-1}, x_j]}(x).\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "\n",
        "  Then $\\|f - f_\\pi\\|_\\infty \\le \\epsilon/2$: for $x \\in (x_{j-1}, x_j]$ we have $|x - x_j| < \\delta$, so $|f(x) - f_\\pi(x)| = |f(x) - f(x_j)| < \\epsilon/2$; and for $x \\notin (x_0, x_k]$ both $f(x)$ and $f_\\pi(x)$ vanish, by condition 3 and $\\text{supp}(f) \\subseteq [a,b]$.\n",
        "  Therefore, for any probability measure $\\nu$:\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "  \\left| \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} f \\, d\\nu - \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} f_\\pi \\, d\\nu \\right| \\le \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} |f - f_\\pi| \\, d\\nu \\le \\frac{\\epsilon}{2}.\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "\n",
        "  Now evaluate $\\int f_\\pi \\, d\\mu_n$:\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "  \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} f_\\pi \\, d\\mu_n = \\sum_{j=1}^k f(x_j) \\mu_n((x_{j-1}, x_j]) = \\sum_{j=1}^k f(x_j) \\big( F_n(x_j) - F_n(x_{j-1}) \\big).\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "\n",
        "  Since $x_j \\in \\text{Cont}(F)$ for all $j$, $F_n(x_j) \\to F(x_j)$ as $n \\to \\infty$. Thus:\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "  \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} f_\\pi \\, d\\mu_n \\xrightarrow{n\\to\\infty} \\sum_{j=1}^k f(x_j) \\big( F(x_j) - F(x_{j-1}) \\big) = \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} f_\\pi \\, d\\mu.\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "\n",
        "  Finally, using the triangle inequality:\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "  \\begin{aligned}\n",
